/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:58:2. Orchestrator spawns a reviewer child with explicit selection, for example `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:59:3. Reviewer child sets/keeps `reviewing`, reads artifacts/code, and writes the verdict.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:61:4a. When the reviewer was launched against a Change Request (a managed Story workspace run), acceptance is recorded by `accept_cr(ID, revision=N, evidence=...)` instead. That mutation sets the Change Request to `accepted`, stamps the reviewer run, and parks the element at `to-review` as the **accepted handoff** — accepted work never stays in `reviewing`. The Orchestrator then runs `task-board worktree checkpoint <ID>` for a **non-final** leaf, which commits the accepted candidate onto the Story branch and makes the `done` transition with `commit_ack=scope_committed`. A Change Request with `repository_delta=empty` skips the commit and still reaches `done` by the same command. `accept_cr` accepts no `commit_ack` argument, its `evidence` must be a task-scoped outcome resource this reviewer run attached or updated (naming the producer's own `_results.md` is refused with `change_request_evidence_missing`), only the reviewer run that was handed the revision may call it (anything else is refused with `change_request_acceptance_unauthorized`), and the live task/reviewer checklist must be complete. All refusals precede CR, status, and activity writes.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:64:5. On changes requested, the Orchestrator preserves the verdict, spawns the appropriate producer, and sends the next `to-review` handoff through a new reviewer. Repeat producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:65:6. If a child exits early or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, the Orchestrator inspects the run/outcomes and retries or reroutes through a focused child. Recoverable work is never handed to the human.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:66:7. A `blocked` verdict stops routing only when it identifies a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision and records the full evidence packet; otherwise retry or reroute.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:68:`reviewing` is a real state, not a pass-through. A reviewer child must do actual review work between the producer handoff and `done`; the orchestrator routes and monitors that review instead of performing the verdict inline.
/Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/references/statuses.md:70:`reviewing` is never a verdict, including under the Change Request flow. A reviewer run that ends at `reviewing` with no accepted Change Request is cancelled and a successor reviewer is spawned — that is what distinguishes "the reviewer decided nothing" from "the reviewer decided", and `accept_cr` does not change it. The accepted branch is derived from the Change Request record, not from the `reviewing` status.
